From: Mirdispo contributors
Subject: Match the card's bitstream to the software one, and say how a picture may be cut

Two encoders were watched against the same receiver and the same link. One
was followed; the other was not, and only ever showed a whole picture when a
keyframe reached it. What they were asked for differed in one way that was
nobody's finding: the card was told to repeat the headers a receiver needs
in order to start reading, with every keyframe, where the software encoder
sends them once.

That was added as something a receiver which cannot ask for anything back
might want. It may well be, but it was never measured, and it is the only
thing one encoder was doing that the other was not. It is dropped, so that
what the two send differs as little as it can while the difference is still
being looked for. Worth returning to deliberately once something is known.

How a picture may be cut into slices has been guessed at twice in the course
of the same question, by two readers who each concluded what suited them and
neither of whom looked. It is the receiver's own statement, it is already in
hand, and it decides what the software encoder is told to do. It is now said
in the log, so it stops being a thing to assume.

diff -ruN a/src/wfd/wfd-media-factory.c b/src/wfd/wfd-media-factory.c
--- a/src/wfd/wfd-media-factory.c
+++ b/src/wfd/wfd-media-factory.c
@@ -306,10 +306,14 @@
                     "qos", TRUE,
                     /* A frame may not wait for one that comes after it. */
                     "zerolatency", TRUE,
-                    /* Sent to a receiver that cannot ask for anything back,
-                     * so the headers it needs to start reading again are
-                     * repeated with every keyframe rather than sent once. */
-                    "repeat-sequence-header", TRUE,
+                    /* Left as the software encoder leaves it. Repeating the
+                     * headers with every keyframe was added here as something
+                     * a receiver that cannot ask for anything back might
+                     * want, but the software encoder does not do it and was
+                     * watched working, so this is one fewer way for the two
+                     * to differ while the difference is still being looked
+                     * for. */
+                    "repeat-sequence-header", FALSE,
                     "preset", 11,  /* p4, medium */
                     "tune", 3,     /* ultra low latency */
                     NULL);
@@ -725,6 +729,13 @@
   g_message ("WfdMediaFactory: %s holding the picture at %u kbit/s with a buffer of %u frames.",
              wfd_gst_elements[encoder_impl], bitrate_kbit, tuning.vbv_frames);
 
+  /* How a picture may be cut up is the receiver's own statement, and what
+   * the software encoder is told to do follows it. It has been guessed at
+   * twice while asking why one encoder suits this receiver and another does
+   * not, so it is said plainly instead. */
+  g_message ("WfdMediaFactory: The receiver takes up to %u slices a picture.",
+             codec->max_slice_num);
+
   if (encoder_impl == ELEMENT_VAAPIH264)
     quirks = WFD_QUIRK_NO_IDR;
 
